<div class="page-header">
  <h2>Network Diagnostics</h2>
  <p class="page-desc">Tests whether this server can make outbound connections to the internet and to each government data source, separate from any search query or API key. There's nothing to "clear" — every load below runs a brand-new, live check; nothing here is cached or stored.</p>
</div>

<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;margin-bottom:.75rem;">
  <a href="/diagnostics/network-check" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm">Run Diagnostics Again</a>
  <span style="font-size:.85rem;color:var(--text-muted);">Checked at: <%= checkedAt.toLocaleString('en-US', { dateStyle: 'medium', timeStyle: 'medium' }) %></span>
</div>

<% if (relayConfigured) { %>
  <p style="font-size:.85rem;background:#f0f7ff;border-left:3px solid #0066cc;border-radius:4px;padding:.6rem .85rem;margin-bottom:.75rem;">
    The SAM.gov Relay row below is what the app actually uses for SAM.gov searches and the daily digest. The <strong>SAM.gov API</strong> row above it tests a <em>direct</em> connection from this server to api.sam.gov, which Hostinger blocks — that row is expected to stay red permanently now that the Relay is in place. It's not a leftover from before the fix; it re-tests and re-fails the same way on every load, since direct access is genuinely still blocked. As long as the Relay row says Reachable, SAM.gov search and the digest are working.
  </p>
<% } %>

<div class="table-wrap">
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Target</th><th>URL</th><th>Result</th><th>Time</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% results.forEach(function(r) { %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= r.label %></td>
          <td><code><%= r.url %></code></td>
          <td>
            <% if (r.ok) { %>
              <span class="badge badge--sent">Reachable</span>
              <% if (r.status !== undefined) { %><small> (HTTP <%= r.status %>)</small><% } %>
            <% } else { %>
              <span class="badge badge--failed">Failed</span><br>
              <small><%= r.error %></small>
            <% } %>
          </td>
          <td><%= r.ms %>ms</td>
        </tr>
      <% }); %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<p style="margin-top:1rem;font-size:.85rem;color:var(--text-muted);">
  If <strong>General internet (Google)</strong> fails too, this server cannot make outbound
  connections at all — that's a Hostinger hosting/firewall setting, not something in the app.
  If Google succeeds but the government API rows fail, those specific hosts are being blocked
  or are unreachable while general internet access works.
</p>
